---
title: "Clustered and stratified data"
description: >
  How to choose what the bootstrap resamples: observations, clusters, two
  cluster dimensions, or units within strata.
---

```{r helpers, include = FALSE}
source(file.path(getOption("bootmakr.precompute.dir"), "helpers.R"))
```

`bootmakr` obtains its standard errors, confidence intervals and *p*-values
by resampling the data. What it resamples is your decision, and it should
follow one rule: **resample the units that you would treat as independent
when choosing standard errors for the regression itself.** If you would
cluster the standard errors by firm, resample firms.

| Your regression would use | Resample | R | Stata |
|---|---|---|---|
| heteroskedasticity-robust standard errors | observations | *(nothing to add)* | *(nothing to add)* |
| standard errors clustered in one dimension | whole clusters | `cluster = "firm"` | `cluster(firm)` |
| standard errors clustered in two dimensions | each dimension and their intersection | `cluster = c("firm", "year")` | not available |
| a stratified sample | within strata | `strata = "region"` | `strata(region)` |
| clusters sampled within strata | clusters within strata | `cluster = "firm", strata = "region"` | `cluster(firm) strata(region)` |

## Observations or clusters

Without `cluster`, each bootstrap sample draws observations with
replacement. This allows the error variance to differ across observations,
the bootstrap counterpart of heteroskedasticity-robust standard errors. It
still assumes that observations are independent.

When observations come in groups (employees in firms, firm-years in firms,
respondents in villages), that assumption fails, and the bootstrap must draw
whole groups. The example of [Get started](bootmakr.html) shows what is at
stake. The data are firm-years, about seven per firm, and whether the CEO
belongs to the owning family varies mostly between firms rather than within
them. Here is the same call twice, once resampling firm-years and once
resampling firms.

`r tabs_r("compare")`

```{r compare, cache = TRUE}
library(bootmakr)
library(haven)

osf  <- "https://osf.io/download/stk7y/?view_only=885d1ed5498c4b3c8a1194882e468286"
chen <- read_dta(osf)

ceo.model <- lg_ceopay ~ owner_ceo + ceo_tenure + PA_nic3_med + ceo_edu_dummy +
  lg_sales + firm_age + promoters_pct + institutions_pct +
  factor(year) + factor(nic_code_1digit)

by.firm.year <- bootmakr(ceo.model, data = chen, treat = "owner_ceo",
                         benchmark_covariates = "firm_age",
                         seed = 123, progress = FALSE)

by.firm <- bootmakr(ceo.model, data = chen, treat = "owner_ceo",
                    benchmark_covariates = "firm_age", cluster = "co_code",
                    seed = 123, progress = FALSE)

rbind("firm-years" = by.firm.year$results,
      "firms"      = by.firm$results)[, c("estimate", "se", "ci_lower", "ci_upper", "pvalue")]
```

`r tabs_stata("compare")`

```{r, echo = FALSE, results = "asis"}
stata_show("first-steps", "data", output = FALSE)
stata_show("first-steps", "controls")
stata_show("first-steps", "compare")
```

`r tabs_end()`

```{r compare-numbers, include = FALSE}
se.obs  <- by.firm.year$results$se
se.firm <- by.firm$results$se
```

The adjusted estimate is the same in both rows, because it is computed from
the original sample. Only its uncertainty changes, and it changes a lot: the
standard error is `r num(se.obs, 3)` when firm-years are resampled and
`r num(se.firm, 3)` when firms are, `r num(se.firm / se.obs, 1)` times as
large. Treating the firm-years as independent overstates how much
information the data hold about family CEOs, in the bootstrap just as in the
regression itself.

The cluster bootstrap needs a reasonable number of clusters, just as
cluster-robust standard errors do. The usual guidance is a few dozen at the
least, and more when clusters differ much in size (Cameron & Miller, 2015).
With few or very unequal clusters its confidence intervals can be too
narrow, and no number of replications repairs that. The simulations in
Lonati and Wulff (2026) document how coverage depends on the number and
balance of clusters.

## Two cluster dimensions

Panel data are often clustered in two ways at once: observations of the same
firm are related, and so are observations of the same year. In R, give
`cluster` two variables. `bootmakr` then runs three bootstraps, one
resampling each dimension and one resampling their intersection, and
combines the three variances as V~firm~ + V~year~ − V~firm×year~, following
Cameron, Gelbach, and Miller (2011).

To see it at work we simulate a small panel in which both the treatment and
the error term have a firm component and a year component.

```{r panel}
set.seed(2026)
firms <- 60
years <- 25
panel <- expand.grid(firm = seq_len(firms), year = seq_len(years))

by.firm <- function() rnorm(firms)[panel$firm]
by.year <- function() rnorm(years)[panel$year]

panel$x <- rnorm(nrow(panel))
panel$d <- 0.5 * panel$x + by.firm() + by.year() + rnorm(nrow(panel))
panel$y <- 0.3 * panel$d + 0.5 * panel$x + by.firm() + by.year() + rnorm(nrow(panel))
```

```{r twoway, cache = TRUE}
one.way <- bootmakr(y ~ d + x, data = panel, treat = "d",
                    benchmark_covariates = "x", cluster = "firm",
                    seed = 123, progress = FALSE)

two.way <- bootmakr(y ~ d + x, data = panel, treat = "d",
                    benchmark_covariates = "x", cluster = c("firm", "year"),
                    seed = 123, progress = FALSE)
two.way
```

```{r twoway-numbers, include = FALSE}
se1 <- one.way$results$se
se2 <- two.way$results$se
```

Clustering by firm alone gives a standard error of `r num(se1, 3)`. Allowing
for the year dimension as well raises it to `r num(se2, 3)`.

A few things differ from the one-way case.

- The result is a variance, not a bootstrap distribution, so the confidence
  interval and the *p*-value use a normal approximation (estimate ± 1.96
  standard errors) instead of percentiles. The output says so.
- `reps` is the number of replications *per* bootstrap, so the run does three
  times the work.
- The combined variance can come out negative in small samples. `bootmakr`
  then falls back on the larger of the two one-way variances and tells you.
- A dimension with very few clusters, such as five years, cannot support
  cluster-robust inference of any kind. `bootmakr` warns when a dimension has
  fewer than ten.
- The draws of the three bootstraps are kept in `$boot_samples$G`, `$H` and
  `$GH`.

Two-way clustering cannot be combined with `strata`, and it is not available
in the Stata command.

## Strata

If the sample was drawn separately within strata (regions, industries, size
classes), the bootstrap should respect that: `strata` resamples within each
stratum, so every bootstrap sample has the same number of units per stratum
as the data. Combined with `cluster`, whole clusters are resampled within
strata.

`r tabs_r("strata")`

```r
bootmakr(y ~ d + x1 + x2, data = dat, treat = "d",
         benchmark_covariates = "x1",
         cluster = "firm", strata = "region")
```

`r tabs_stata("strata")`

```stata
bootmakr y d x1 x2, treat(d) benchmark(x1) cluster(firm) strata(region)
```

`r tabs_end()`

## References

Cameron, A. C., Gelbach, J. B., & Miller, D. L. (2011). Robust inference with
multiway clustering. *Journal of Business & Economic Statistics*, 29(2),
238--249. <https://doi.org/10.1198/jbes.2010.07136>

Cameron, A. C., & Miller, D. L. (2015). A practitioner's guide to
cluster-robust inference. *Journal of Human Resources*, 50(2), 317--372.
<https://doi.org/10.3368/jhr.50.2.317>

Chen, G., Chittoor, R., & Vissa, B. (2021). Does nepotism run in the family?
CEO pay and pay-performance sensitivity in Indian family firms. *Strategic
Management Journal*, 42(7), 1326--1343. <https://doi.org/10.1002/smj.3263>

Lonati, S., & Wulff, J. N. (2026). *Why you should not use the ITCV with
robust standard errors (and what to do instead)*. Working paper.
